    {
      "id": "CTN-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "The relative ideal norm and the prime-to-ℓ exponent in both identities",
      "anchor": "inputs-tower-norm-degree",
      "statement": "At the compatible primes over r∤qN, the relative ideal norm raises the lower prime to s=ord_qN(r)/ord_N(r). In the selected family this is prime to ℓ. The element X_d^s solves the signed ideal and conjugation conditions for that ideal and is used in both the norm relation and the congruence at all primes over q.",
      "hypotheses": "N=Fd, q prime, q and d coprime, qd squarefree, r prime and r≡1 modulo F. Each auxiliary prime p dividing qd is 1 modulo F, differs from r and has ℓ∤ord_p(r). The element comparison uses 0≤b<F and a common choice of place above r. The generic relative-norm supplier assumes only the upper field is cyclotomic and states a divisibility result. The displayed ratio uses cyclotomicity of both the lower and upper fields in this application.",
